Kaminagara: is the physical manifestation of a Throne God's Hegemony Law. It exists as a universe/multiverse within them, but can be brought out if they desire it. Kaminagara are typically absolutely massive in size as they themselves are walking universes. Inside the Kaminagara is the Law of its God, lacking any blind spots. Damaging Kaminagara will effect the main God to a limited extend, comparable to a venomless snake bite. That being said, only a Law can challenge a Law on equal metaphysical terms. Reinhardt’s Kaminagara manifests as an overwhelming, radiant embodiment of eternal warfare, taking the form of an immense, monstrous golden lion whose colossal presence dominates the surrounding world. Its enormous head possesses a ferocious, almost demonic appearance, with a broad leonine muzzle, deep-set glowing eyes, and a cavernous mouth filled with multiple rows of enormous, irregular, razor-sharp fangs, including particularly prominent upper canines that curve downward like ivory sabers. Its jaws are spread wide in a thunderous roar, exposing a massive tongue and the dark recesses of its throat, while thick layers of golden fur frame its face and extend across its powerful neck. Rising from its head and shoulders is an extraordinary mane composed of countless enormous, wildly flowing locks of golden and amber fur, resembling a blazing solar corona as they spread outward in every direction. Brilliant yellow light courses through the mane, illuminating its individual strands and making the entire creature appear to be engulfed in living golden flames. The lion's gigantic body stretches beyond the visible scale, its immense proportions suggesting a presence far greater than the figures gathered around it. Surrounding this monstrous manifestation are the members of Reinhardt’s legion, appearing as spectral yet distinctly recognizable figures dressed in dark military uniforms, long coats, ceremonial attire, and combat equipment. Their expressions range from bloodthirsty exhilaration and manic laughter to cold, composed determination, while their bodies emerge from the brilliant golden radiance as though they are inseparable components of the same divine existence and the forefront stands Reinhardt himself. Behind and around him, enormous golden, flame-like extensions and luminous currents sweep across the composition, visually connecting his figure with the gigantic lion and the countless warriors accompanying him. The entire manifestation is immersed in an incandescent atmosphere of gold, amber, black, and white, with innumerable sparks and particles drifting through the surrounding darkness. The contrast between the brilliant, almost sacred golden illumination and the ferocious expressions of the lion and its warriors creates an image of divine majesty intertwined with unrestrained bloodlust. Rather than appearing as a solitary beast, Reinhardt’s Kaminagara presents itself as a vast, unified manifestation of The Holy Mephistopheles and his eternal army, with Reinhard standing at its heart as the sovereign who commands and embraces the endless battlefield.

WEAKNESSES
Existence Is Bound to Mercurius:
Reinhardt’s greatest existential weakness is his nature as the True Death Cell of Mercurius. He is the living manifestation of Mercurius's subconscious desire for self-destruction, a being whose existence is fundamentally tied to the god who produced him. This relationship grants Reinhardt extraordinary advantages, including the ability to grow powerful enough to confront his originator and a form of immortality that prevents his permanent death while Mercurius remains alive. However, this protection also establishes a fatal dependency: should Mercurius truly perish, Reinhardt will perish alongside him. Destroying Reinhardt’s body, soul, or divine manifestation is therefore not necessarily sufficient to end his existence permanently, but eliminating Mercurius under circumstances that prevent the continuation of their shared existence can bring Reinhardt to an end regardless of his own remaining strength. Reinhardt will fade away when Mercurius finally receives the death he desires. His immense power does not free him from this relationship, and his status as a True Death Cell prevents him from independently inheriting 「The Throne」 under those circumstances.

Restricted Full Power Against Opponents Other Than Mercurius: Reinhardt’s status as Mercurius's True Death Cell creates a distinction between his general divine power and the extraordinary strength he can bring to bear against his originator, which explains his incomplete throne godhood. A True Death Cell possesses an inherent ability to overcome the disparity in power separating it from the god responsible for its existence. It specifically describes Reinhardt as unable to employ his full Throne God power against opponents other than Mercurius. The important distinction is that this limitation does not make him powerless, weak, or incapable of fighting other transcendent beings. Rather, the special relationship that enables him to contend with Mercurius is not a universal ability to equalize his power against any opponent.

Dependence on His Legion for the Full Expression of His Law: Reinhardt’s Hegemony Law, Promised Evil Days of Endless War Comings, is inseparable from the idea of eternal battle and the warriors gathered beneath him. His Legion is not simply a collection of disposable soldiers: its souls and warriors form an integral part of his supernatural existence and the expression of his craving. Through his Hegemony Law, Reinhardt incorporates souls into his army and empowers his Fings, creating the military manifestation of his desire for a world of endless warfare. His vast accumulation of souls contributes to his power, and his Legion serves as the principal expression of his divine dominion. Consequently, depriving Reinhardt of his Legion would interfere with a fundamental aspect of his abilities. It explicitly characterizes the army as necessary for displaying his full power, although that interpretation should not be confused with a demonstrated method of defeating him by simply killing individual soldiers even though every soldier is technically immortal and cannot be killed through conventional senses. His Legion is part of his divine system, and its relationship with his soul makes its complete elimination substantially different from destroying a conventional army. 

Separation from the Ordinary World While Within the Singular Finite Curve: Reinhardt’s supernatural influence is not necessarily capable of affecting every region of existence irrespective of metaphysical boundaries. The Singular Finite Curve, which can lead not only the Root but mainly towards 「The Throne」, is separated from ordinary creation. Actions occurring within it do not automatically influence the external world, and accounts of Reinhardt’s passage through this domain specifically note that his influence does not reach individuals while he remains inside. This represents a spatial and metaphysical limitation rather than a conventional range restriction on his spear or physical attacks. His power remains enormous within the Singular Finite Curve, but the separation between that domain and ordinary creation prevents its effects from simply propagating everywhere without regard for the structure of the setting. This matters when evaluating scenarios involving intervention from beyond creation, protection of external targets, or the supposed omnipresence of his Hegemony Law.

Inability to Permanently Escape Mercurius's Eternal Recurrence Through Victory Alone: Even Reinhardt’s ability to defeat Mercurius does not automatically guarantee liberation from the Heaven Lost. Mercurius's desire creates an endlessly recurring universe, and his Hegemony Law can reset existence even after events that would ordinarily constitute a decisive defeat. The history of 「The Throne」 includes repeated iterations in which Mercurius is defeated by Reinhardt, only for the world to return to the beginning. The permanent termination of recurrence requires the fulfillment of Mercurius's particular desire involving Void Shiki rather than merely inflicting sufficient physical or divine damage upon him. This creates a crucial limitation on Reinhardt’s capacity to accomplish his ultimate objective independently. He may possess the power to challenge and defeat his originator, but a victorious battle is not necessarily equivalent to permanently destroying the system that sustains their conflict. This vulnerability concerns the victory conditions imposed by Mercurius's Hegemony Law rather than a simple deficiency in Reinhardt’s offensive power. 


